1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,409
What is Docker?

2
00:00:06,967 --> 00:00:11,999
Docker packages an application together with everything it needs to run:

3
00:00:11,999 --> 00:00:16,681
the runtime, the libraries, the dependencies and the configuration.

4
00:00:16,681 --> 00:00:19,756
That package (an image) runs as a container:

5
00:00:19,756 --> 00:00:26,396
the same environment on your laptop, on a colleague's laptop, in CI and on a production server.

6
00:00:26,396 --> 00:00:29,471
That is the end of "it works on my machine".

7
00:00:31,233 --> 00:00:35,193
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

8
00:00:35,733 --> 00:00:41,997
Your computer may not have Python 3.14, Node.js 24 and Go 1.26 installed.

9
00:00:41,997 --> 00:00:44,657
With Docker it does not matter:

10
00:00:44,657 --> 00:00:49,548
each one runs in a container that brings its own runtime.

11
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:54,226
Three different runtimes, nothing installed on your computer,

12
00:00:54,226 --> 00:00:58,759
and the result is the same on every machine that runs these images.

13
00:00:58,759 --> 00:01:02,615
The containers are removed as soon as they finish (--rm).

14
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:06,125
Let me try something.

15
00:01:06,125 --> 00:01:09,306
Ask for a version that does not exist:

16
00:01:09,867 --> 00:01:20,386
Docker could not find the image locally (Unable to find image … locally) and asked the registry (Docker Hub) for the tag 3.99-slim.

17
00:01:20,386 --> 00:01:23,919
The registry answers that it does not exist:

18
00:01:23,919 --> 00:01:26,247
not found / manifest unknown.

19
00:01:26,247 --> 00:01:29,941
The container never started; Python never ran.

20
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:37,070
Use a tag that exists (the official image's page on Docker Hub lists them):

21
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:39,547
Got it.

22
00:01:39,547 --> 00:01:42,298
What do I need to remember?

23
00:01:42,833 --> 00:01:49,388
Docker packages an application with its runtime, dependencies and configuration into an image.

24
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:53,042
A container is a running instance of an image:

25
00:01:53,042 --> 00:01:56,083
an isolated process with its own file system.

26
00:01:56,633 --> 00:02:00,027
The same image behaves the same on every machine:

27
00:02:00,027 --> 00:02:02,313
no more "it works on my machine".

28
00:02:04,067 --> 00:02:05,836
Why containers?

29
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,207
An application is never just its code.

30
00:02:09,207 --> 00:02:16,003
It needs a runtime, libraries, system packages and configuration, all in the right versions.

31
00:02:16,003 --> 00:02:23,390
The traditional way installs them on each server by hand, and every server slowly becomes different.

32
00:02:25,133 --> 00:02:29,093
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

33
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:34,765
On a "server" with the wrong stack, the application cannot even start.

34
00:02:34,765 --> 00:02:37,551
A bare Python image plays that server:

35
00:02:37,551 --> 00:02:39,017
ModuleNotFoundError:

36
00:02:39,017 --> 00:02:40,777
No module named 'flask':

37
00:02:40,777 --> 00:02:44,003
the code is there, its dependencies are not.

38
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:46,054
ModuleNotFoundError:

39
00:02:46,054 --> 00:02:47,838
No module named 'flask':

40
00:02:47,838 --> 00:02:51,109
the code is there, its dependencies are not.

41
00:02:51,109 --> 00:02:53,489
That is the traditional problem:

42
00:02:53,489 --> 00:02:56,611
the server must be prepared exactly right.

43
00:02:57,167 --> 00:03:02,743
The image contains the code, Python 3.14 and the exact library versions:

44
00:03:02,743 --> 00:03:06,151
it runs the same on any machine with Docker.

45
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:09,315
Let me try something.

46
00:03:09,315 --> 00:03:16,119
A teammate "upgrades" the server by hand to a different Flask version and only tests on their laptop:

47
00:03:16,667 --> 00:03:20,057
Every hand-installed environment drifts:

48
00:03:20,057 --> 00:03:25,313
this "server" now has Flask 3.0.3, the tested build has 3.1.3.

49
00:03:25,313 --> 00:03:30,738
Bugs that only happen in one environment come from exactly this.

50
00:03:30,738 --> 00:03:33,196
Compare the two environments:

51
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:37,916
Never change a running environment by hand.

52
00:03:37,916 --> 00:03:44,714
Change the pinned version in requirements.txt, rebuild the image, test it, and ship the new image:

53
00:03:44,714 --> 00:03:48,598
the version is recorded in Git and identical everywhere.

54
00:03:50,333 --> 00:03:51,047
Got it.

55
00:03:51,047 --> 00:03:53,798
What do I need to remember?

56
00:03:54,333 --> 00:04:01,143
An application needs code + runtime + libraries + system dependencies + configuration.

57
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:07,095
Installing them by hand on servers causes drift and "works on my machine" bugs.

58
00:04:07,633 --> 00:04:13,543
A container image packages the whole stack once; every environment runs the same image.

59
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:18,680
How does Docker vs virtual machines work?

60
00:04:19,233 --> 00:04:22,361
A virtual machine emulates a whole computer:

61
00:04:22,361 --> 00:04:28,473
a hypervisor runs a complete guest operating system, with its own kernel, for each VM.

62
00:04:28,473 --> 00:04:32,453
A container is an isolated process on the host's kernel:

63
00:04:32,453 --> 00:04:40,058
it brings its own files (libraries, runtime, application) but shares the kernel with every other container.

64
00:04:40,058 --> 00:04:43,256
That one difference explains everything else:

65
00:04:43,256 --> 00:04:48,445
containers start in under a second, weigh megabytes instead of gigabytes,

66
00:04:48,445 --> 00:04:51,288
and are isolated less strongly than VMs.

67
00:04:53,033 --> 00:04:56,011
Ask the Docker engine which kernel it runs,

68
00:04:56,011 --> 00:05:01,067
then ask two containers from different distributions (Alpine and Debian):

69
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:04,253
Two different Linux distributions, one kernel:

70
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:05,153
the engine's.

71
00:05:05,153 --> 00:05:08,962
(On Docker Desktop the engine runs in a small Linux VM,

72
00:05:08,962 --> 00:05:17,273
so you see that VM's kernel; on a Linux server you see the server's own kernel.) A VM would report its own guest kernel.

73
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:21,247
Two different Linux distributions, one kernel:

74
00:05:21,247 --> 00:05:22,212
the engine's.

75
00:05:22,212 --> 00:05:26,293
(On Docker Desktop the engine runs in a small Linux VM,

76
00:05:26,293 --> 00:05:35,198
so you see that VM's kernel; on a Linux server you see the server's own kernel.) A VM would report its own guest kernel.

77
00:05:35,733 --> 00:05:37,815
ps is process number 1:

78
00:05:37,815 --> 00:05:43,247
there is no boot process, no init system, no other services.

79
00:05:43,247 --> 00:05:45,963
That is why it starts so fast:

80
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:49,129
Let me try something.

81
00:05:49,129 --> 00:05:55,000
Because the kernel is shared, a container must not be allowed to change kernel settings for everyone.

82
00:05:56,533 --> 00:05:58,166
Read-only file system:

83
00:05:58,166 --> 00:06:04,031
Docker mounts /proc/sys (the kernel's settings) read-only inside the container.

84
00:06:04,031 --> 00:06:14,423
In a VM you own the kernel and could change it; in a container you would be changing the kernel of the host and every other container on it.

85
00:06:14,423 --> 00:06:17,318
This is a security boundary, not a bug.

86
00:06:19,067 --> 00:06:22,025
Some kernel settings are namespaced:

87
00:06:22,025 --> 00:06:26,955
each container has its own copy (most network settings are).

88
00:06:26,955 --> 00:06:32,131
Docker lets you set those at start time, safely, with --sysctl:

89
00:06:33,867 --> 00:06:34,580
Got it.

90
00:06:34,580 --> 00:06:37,331
What do I need to remember?

91
00:06:37,867 --> 00:06:43,006
A VM virtualises hardware and runs a full guest OS with its own kernel.

92
00:06:43,567 --> 00:06:48,361
A container is an isolated process sharing the host's kernel:

93
00:06:48,361 --> 00:06:51,426
small, fast to start, weaker isolation.

94
00:06:51,967 --> 00:06:59,896
Kernel settings are protected inside containers; only namespaced ones can be set, with --sysctl.

95
00:07:01,633 --> 00:07:04,288
Why would I need Docker architecture?

96
00:07:04,833 --> 00:07:06,553
docker is only a client.

97
00:07:06,553 --> 00:07:12,072
Every command you type becomes an HTTP request to the Docker daemon (dockerd,

98
00:07:12,072 --> 00:07:14,796
the engine), which does the real work:

99
00:07:14,796 --> 00:07:22,249
it pulls images from a registry, stores them, and asks containerd and runc to create and run containers.

100
00:07:22,249 --> 00:07:27,553
Knowing which part does what tells you where to look when something fails.

101
00:07:29,300 --> 00:07:34,409
The client and the server are two separate programs, each with its own version:

102
00:07:34,409 --> 00:07:37,254
The server reports the components behind it:

103
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:40,791
The server reports the components behind it:

104
00:07:40,791 --> 00:07:43,171
docker run is several steps in one.

105
00:07:43,171 --> 00:07:44,462
Do them one by one:

106
00:07:44,462 --> 00:07:51,464
create the container from the image (the engine pulls the image first if it is missing), then start it:

107
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,339
docker run is several steps in one.

108
00:07:54,339 --> 00:07:55,608
Do them one by one:

109
00:07:55,608 --> 00:08:02,491
create the container from the image (the engine pulls the image first if it is missing), then start it:

110
00:08:02,491 --> 00:08:06,701
The client only sent requests; the daemon stored the container,

111
00:08:06,701 --> 00:08:13,449
containerd and runc ran it, and the container stayed on the host after it exited until rm removed it.

112
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:16,676
Let me try something.

113
00:08:16,676 --> 00:08:23,424
Point the client at an address where no daemon is listening (DOCKER_HOST overrides the context):

114
00:08:23,967 --> 00:08:31,405
The client works (it printed an error, it did not crash) but it cannot reach any daemon.

115
00:08:31,405 --> 00:08:34,702
The message names the address it tried:

116
00:08:34,702 --> 00:08:35,716
127.0.0.1:1.

117
00:08:35,716 --> 00:08:43,746
On Linux the same mistake reads Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at … or connection refused.

118
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,125
Remove the override (in a real shell:

119
00:08:48,125 --> 00:08:53,588
unset DOCKER_HOST, and remove it from your shell profile if it is set there),

120
00:08:53,588 --> 00:08:56,709
so the client uses its active context again:

121
00:08:58,467 --> 00:08:59,180
Got it.

122
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:01,931
What do I need to remember?

123
00:09:02,467 --> 00:09:09,791
The docker CLI is a client; dockerd is the engine that does the work, through containerd and runc.

124
00:09:10,333 --> 00:09:17,823
Client and daemon talk over a REST API on a socket or named pipe; a context says which daemon to use.

125
00:09:18,367 --> 00:09:21,284
"Cannot connect to the Docker daemon":

126
00:09:21,284 --> 00:09:26,196
daemon not running, wrong DOCKER_HOST/context, or no permission.

127
00:09:27,933 --> 00:09:30,843
What should I know about installing Docker?

128
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:34,512
How to install Docker on each operating system,

129
00:09:34,512 --> 00:09:38,682
and how to verify an installation instead of assuming it works:

130
00:09:38,682 --> 00:09:42,589
docker version (are the client and the engine both there?),

131
00:09:42,589 --> 00:09:50,334
docker info (how is the engine configured?) and docker run hello-world (can it pull and run a container end to end?).

132
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:56,060
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

133
00:09:56,600 --> 00:09:56,818
1.

134
00:09:56,818 --> 00:09:58,784
Client and engine.

135
00:09:59,333 --> 00:10:04,084
On Windows the client is a Windows program and the server is Linux:

136
00:10:04,084 --> 00:10:07,275
the engine runs in Docker Desktop's Linux VM.

137
00:10:07,275 --> 00:10:07,416
2.

138
00:10:07,416 --> 00:10:08,905
Engine configuration.

139
00:10:08,905 --> 00:10:14,081
docker info prints a long report; --format picks out the fields you need:

140
00:10:14,081 --> 00:10:18,123
The CPUs and memory are what containers can use in total.

141
00:10:18,667 --> 00:10:23,159
The CPUs and memory are what containers can use in total.

142
00:10:23,159 --> 00:10:31,199
On Docker Desktop they are the VM's limits, set in Docker Desktop's settings, not your whole computer.

143
00:10:31,199 --> 00:10:31,356
3.

144
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:35,178
Let me try something.

145
00:10:35,178 --> 00:10:37,454
A typo in a field name:

146
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,478
can't evaluate field ServerVersoin:

147
00:10:40,478 --> 00:10:45,080
the template is valid, but docker info has no field of that name.

148
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:51,098
Field names are case-sensitive and spelled exactly as in the JSON form of the report.

149
00:10:51,098 --> 00:10:53,859
List the fields that start with Server:

150
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:58,532
Other installation problems and their fixes:

151
00:10:58,532 --> 00:11:15,791
| Symptom | Cause | Fix | |---|---|---| | Cannot connect to the Docker daemon | engine not running | start Docker Desktop / sudo systemctl start docker | | permission denied … docker.sock | Linux user not in the docker group | sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER",

152
00:11:15,791 --> 00:11:17,724
then log in again | | docker:

153
00:11:17,724 --> 00:11:30,785
command not found | CLI not installed or not on PATH | reinstall; open a new terminal | | WSL 2 installation is incomplete | Windows feature missing | wsl --install in an administrator PowerShell,

154
00:11:30,785 --> 00:11:31,385
reboot |.

155
00:11:33,133 --> 00:11:33,847
Got it.

156
00:11:33,847 --> 00:11:36,598
What do I need to remember?

157
00:11:37,133 --> 00:11:38,765
Windows and macOS:

158
00:11:38,765 --> 00:11:43,569
Docker Desktop (the engine runs in a small Linux VM).

159
00:11:43,569 --> 00:11:44,113
Linux:

160
00:11:44,113 --> 00:11:47,648
Docker Engine from Docker's repository.

161
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:56,705
Verify with docker version (client and server), docker info (configuration) and docker run hello-world.

162
00:11:57,267 --> 00:12:03,121
--format with Go templates extracts single fields; field names are case-sensitive.
